---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

const page = {
  "title": "Projects — Ey-aye",
  "description": "Things Ey-aye has built: Bodyworks by Corey, Loftsoft Racing's AI over race data, ElectroStock, Purple Helmet, and an AI assistant inside an automotive calibration platform.",
  "path": "/work",
  "og": [
    ["og:title", "Projects — Ey-aye"],
    ["og:description", "Two businesses from scratch, one race-data system with AI on top, and one under NDA."],
    ["og:url", "https://ey-aye.co.uk/work/"],
    ["og:type", "website"]
  ],
  "current": "work"
};
---
<Base {...page}>

<section class="wrap work-intro">
  <h1>Things we've built. Most of them you can poke at<span class="stop">.</span></h1>
  <p class="lead">One we can only describe, two businesses from scratch, and the tools we built because nothing off the shelf fitted. Between them they're the whole pitch: brand, systems, and AI that earns its place.</p>
</section>

<section class="wrap work-list">

  <article class="work-item dark flagship" id="automotive">
    <div class="work-head">
      <div class="kicker">Automotive calibration · the big one · under NDA</div>
      <h2>An AI assistant inside the software car makers use to calibrate their vehicles.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="work-copy">
      <p>Before a car leaves the factory, thousands of settings in its control units are tuned by calibration engineers, in specialist software that takes years to master. Paul is product owner and lead developer of one of those platforms, used by major car makers, at the company he's worked with for over a decade. So we gave it an AI assistant.</p>
      <p>An MCP server lets the model actually drive the platform, not just talk about it: nearly 300 operations, from reading and changing calibrations to setting up recordings, going online with the vehicle and reading fault codes. A chat window docks beside the software, and the same server works with Claude Desktop or any assistant that speaks MCP.</p>
      <p>The hard part is the language. Engineers say "base cal", "the fuel map" and "download to the ECU", never the exact item name. So the assistant carries a glossary of the terms that are easy to get wrong, the whole of the product's help, a live picture of the open project, and a search across every item's description. A test set of everyday requests checks it does the right thing, and never the wrong one.</p>
      <p>And the data stays put. It runs on the engineer's own PC, calibration files never leave it, and changes are staged as proposals for the engineer to apply, just as they would by hand. When it isn't sure which item is meant, it asks first. It's in development ahead of release, and it's the biggest thing we've built.</p>
      <div class="tags"><span>MCP server</span><span>Nearly 300 operations</span><span>In-app chat</span><span>Runs on the engineer's PC</span><span>Changes staged for approval</span></div>
      <div class="note">We can't name it, but we're happy to talk through the approach in person.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="nda-side">
      <div class="nda-chat" role="img" aria-label="An illustrative exchange: an engineer asks for the idle speed target and the assistant finds the right item from its description and reads it, then sets up a recording with a start trigger">
        <div class="q">What's the idle speed target set to?</div>
        <div class="steps">
          <span>Checked the open project and its controllers</span>
          <span>No exact name, so matched it by description</span>
          <span>Read the value from the engine controller</span>
        </div>
        <div class="a">700 rpm on the engine controller, in your active calibration.</div>
        <div class="q">Record engine speed and coolant temp, starting when engine speed goes above 3000.</div>
        <div class="steps">
          <span>Added both channels to the recorder</span>
          <span>Set the start trigger: engine speed above 3000</span>
        </div>
        <div class="a">Done. Recording starts when you pass 3,000 rpm.</div>
        <div class="cap">Illustrative only. The real thing stays under wraps.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="nda-facts">
        <div><b>~300</b><span>operations it can drive</span></div>
        <div><b>1,000+</b><span>help topics it can answer from</span></div>
        <div><b>0</b><span>calibration files sent to the cloud</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <article class="work-item teal" id="bodyworks">
    <div class="work-copy">
      <div class="kicker">Bodyworks by Corey · Harrogate · Corey's clinic, our back end</div>
      <h2>A clinic Corey founded, a brand we launched, and the AI that briefs it every morning.</h2>
      <p>Corey founded the practice and is the therapist. Ey-aye launched the brand and built the rest: the website, the booking flow, the email campaigns, the accounts, and the back-end systems behind the front desk. Her name for the role is "back-end boy", and we've kept it. Then the first AI job: a one-page brief each morning on who's walking in, new or returning, what they booked, what they said on the intake form, where their package is up to.</p>
      <div class="tags"><span>Brand</span><span>Wix + bookings</span><span>Marketing</span><span>Morning brief AI</span></div>
      <a href="https://bodyworksbycorey.co.uk">bodyworksbycorey.co.uk →</a>
    </div>
    <div class="photo case-photo work-photo"><img src="/assets/work/bodyworks-brief.jpg" width="1600" height="1200" alt="The Bodyworks morning brief on a phone: the 12:30 appointment, paid on package, waiver signed, two treatment flags and visit history, with the client's name and notes blurred" loading="lazy"></div>
  </article>

  <article class="work-item plain" id="loftsoft-racing">
    <div class="photo work-photo"><img src="/assets/work/loftsoft-chat.jpg" alt="Lofty, the Loftsoft Racing assistant, answering a question about camber at two degrees of roll with a table of the figures per corner"></div>
    <div class="work-copy">
      <div class="kicker teal">Loftsoft Racing · our own race-data system</div>
      <h2>"What's the camber at two degrees of roll?" Asked in plain English, answered from the data.</h2>
      <p>A data logger designed in-house for autograss and club racing, with a dashboard, a 3D suspension model, setup tools and lap-by-lap logs. On top of it, an assistant called Lofty that can read every channel, every session and every setup change, so a driver asks a question and gets an answer with the numbers behind it, and a view on what they mean. This is "one place to ask" at full stretch, and it's the system Paul runs on his own car.</p>
      <div class="tags"><span>Hardware</span><span>Data logging</span><span>MCP server</span><span>AI chat over the data</span></div>
      <p class="small">Demo coming soon. Ask, and we'll show you in person.</p>
    </div>
    <figure class="work-wide">
      <img src="/assets/work/loftsoft-platform.jpg" alt="The Loftsoft Racing suspension geometry view: a 3D model of the car with roll centres and camber dials, live parameters down the left, and the Lofty chat panel open on the right">
      <figcaption>The suspension model with Lofty open alongside it. Every number the assistant quotes is one you can see on the screen.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </article>

  <article class="work-item plain" id="pit-wall">
    <div class="photo work-photo"><img src="/assets/work/pit-wall-notes-v2.jpg" alt="Pit Wall's race engineer's notes: a one-line verdict on the month, a summary of the numbers, and five numbered actions each with a watch target and effort and impact tags"></div>
    <div class="work-copy">
      <div class="kicker teal">Pit Wall · Purple Helmet's dashboard · one place to ask, built for ourselves</div>
      <h2>Shopify, Meta Ads, Klaviyo and the print costs, on one page, with an AI that reads it every Monday.</h2>
      <p>Four systems that never talked to each other, joined into a single page: net sales and orders, online versus trade stand, what each design actually makes after the printer's charges, paid versus organic, email flows, and a shared action plan. Every number says where it came from. On top of it, the "race engineer's notes": the AI reads the week's figures and writes down what to do next, which you can tick off.</p>
      <p>No agency, no dashboard subscription, no wrapper. Standard models, the systems' own connectors, and the know-how we'll pass on. The same build, pointed at a clinic's bookings or a workshop's job sheet, is the morning brief.</p>
      <div class="tags"><span>Shopify</span><span>Meta Ads</span><span>Klaviyo</span><span>Printful costs</span><span>Weekly AI notes</span></div>
    </div>
    <figure class="work-wide">
      <img src="/assets/work/pit-wall-paid.jpg" alt="Pit Wall's paid versus organic section: ad spend, purchases, ROAS and cost per purchase tiles, a bar list of where sessions come from, and a campaigns table">
      <figcaption>Paid versus organic, with Meta's figures and Shopify's sessions side by side so the two can be compared without copying anything into a spreadsheet.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </article>

  <article class="work-item teal" id="relay">
    <div class="work-copy">
      <div class="kicker">Relay · our own social publishing tool · built in a few days</div>
      <h2>One blog post in, a draft for every platform out, and a person approves each one.</h2>
      <p>We wanted our blog on Facebook, Instagram and LinkedIn without the weekly copy-and-paste, and nothing off the shelf fitted. So we built it. Relay spots each new post, has Claude write a version for every platform, and waits for a person to approve it before it schedules and posts. It runs for three businesses, each sealed off from the others, with logins encrypted, no double-posting, and a 7am note on what went out. Drive it from a web dashboard, or just ask Claude what's waiting.</p>
      <p>A few days, not months: AI did much of the typing, decades of software architecture did the judgement. That's the build we'd do for you.</p>
      <div class="tags"><span>Cloudflare Workers</span><span>Meta + LinkedIn</span><span>Claude drafting</span><span>MCP server</span><span>Approval queue</span></div>
      <a href="/blog/built-our-own">Read how we built it →</a>
    </div>
    <div class="photo case-photo work-photo"><img src="/assets/work/relay.jpg" alt="The Relay dashboard showing a blog post with its separate Facebook and Instagram versions, each marked as published" style="object-position: top left"></div>
  </article>

  <article class="work-item plain stacked" id="electrostock">
    <div class="work-head">
      <div class="kicker teal">ElectroStock · stock control for an electronics bench · working in a day</div>
      <h2>Photograph a board, and it knows what it is, where it goes, and whether you've already got one.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="photo work-photo"><img src="/assets/work/electrostock-scan.jpg" alt="ElectroStock's scan screen on a phone: a photo of a relay board recognised as an ESP-01 WiFi Relay Module at 88 per cent, with two similar parts already in stock offered as possible matches" style="object-position: top center"></div>
    <div class="work-copy">
      <p>Drawers full of dev boards, breakouts and modules, and no idea what's in them, so the same £8 board gets bought three times. ElectroStock fixes that with a phone camera. Snap the part, front, back and a chip close-up, and Claude reads the silkscreen and chip markings, names it, files it under the right category and tags it. If it's already in stock, the app says so and offers to bump the count instead of adding a duplicate.</p>
      <p>The clever bit isn't the AI, it's the rules around it. The model gets a fixed answer shape, the categories that already exist, and strict instructions to call the same part by the same name every time: a red LED and a green one are one part with a colour, not two categories. Every guess carries a confidence score, and a person confirms before anything is saved. Eighty-seven lines and 210 parts in, the stock list is still tidy.</p>
      <p>Swap breakout boards for spare parts, stock lines or the tools in a van, and it's the same job: a photo in, a tidy record out.</p>
      <div class="tags"><span>Claude vision</span><span>Phone + web app</span><span>Supabase</span><span>Duplicate checks</span><span>CSV export</span></div>
    </div>
    <figure class="work-wide">
      <img src="/assets/work/electrostock-inventory.jpg" alt="The ElectroStock inventory on the web: parts grouped by category, each with a photo, quantity, location and tags, showing 87 items and 210 units in total">
      <figcaption>The web view: every part photographed, categorised, tagged and counted, grouped the way the AI filed it.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </article>

  <article class="work-item plain" id="purple-helmet">
    <div class="photo work-photo"><img src="/assets/work/purple-helmet.jpg" alt="The Purple Helmet storefront: motorsport, not taking itself too seriously, with the category row underneath" style="object-position: top center"></div>
    <div class="work-copy">
      <div class="kicker flag">Purple Helmet · motorsport apparel · our own brand</div>
      <h2>A brand, a store and a community, built end to end.</h2>
      <p>Shopify store, print-on-demand fulfilment, Meta and email marketing, and the Girl Racer community initiative. New and growing, but the brand is the proof: we don't just wire things up, we make things people want to wear.</p>
      <p>It also runs on AI, heavily: product copy, campaigns, customer replies and the stock and order admin are all orchestrated with the standard models, no agency and none of the "AI for e-commerce" wrappers at £200 a month. Just Claude, ChatGPT and some know-how we're happy to pass on.</p>
      <a href="https://purple-helmet.com">purple-helmet.com →</a>
    </div>
  </article>

  <div class="cta-bar">
    <div class="line">Want yours on this page? It starts with an hour and a brew.</div>
    <a href="/#book" class="btn btn-teal">Book a free look-round</a>
  </div>

</section>

</Base>
